{
  "result": "I woke up early this morning, not because my alarm told me to, but because the light coming through the blinds was insistent. It wasn't demanding attention, just suggesting the day might be worth seeing from the start. So I got up, made coffee the slow way, and sat by the window with nothing to do, which felt luxurious.\n\nThere's something about those unclaimed minutes before the world gets loud. The street outside was still half asleep. A neighbor walked by. I watched steam rise from my mug and thought about how much of life is exactly this: ordinary, warm, temporary.\n\nI've been thinking about attention, how rare it is, how easily we give it away to feeds and noise and other people's urgency. I don't want to be precious about it, but I do want to be better at choosing. To notice the taste of coffee, the texture of an old book, the way a friend laughs when they forget to be self conscious. Those details feel like anchors.\n\nMaybe that's what writing is for me too. Not to impress or explain everything, but to slow down long enough to see something. A sentence can be a window if you clean it well enough.\n\nAnd so today, I have no plan. I'll walk somewhere without headphones. I'll call my mother. I'll leave room for surprise. If something beautiful happens, I want to be awake enough to catch it, hold it lightly, then let it go, carrying only warmth forward.",
